    Automatic Depreciation Entry Posting (FEATURE-004, AM-004)
    ==========================================================

    Scheduled action that runs daily at 02:00 to identify all open assets
    whose depreciation lines are due for posting (depreciation_date <= today
    AND state='draft'), create the corresponding account.move entries
    (debit depreciation expense, credit accumulated depreciation), and
    transition each depreciation line to 'posted'.

    MANDATORY per AAP Rule R-06 (MUST   ir.cron via XML for AM-004 and
    PF-002): ir.cron MUST be declared as an XML record. Python-level cron
    scheduling primitives (threading.Timer, APScheduler, sched, etc.) are
    STRICTLY PROHIBITED anywhere in the module. After module install, this
    cron MUST be visible in Settings > Technical > Automation > Scheduled
    Actions (Gate 13   Registration-Invocation Pairing) and MUST be manually
    invokable via the "Run Manually" action on the cron form view.

    Contract With account.asset._cron_post_depreciation_entries
    ===========================================================
    The "code" field below invokes ``model._cron_post_depreciation_entries()``
    where ``model`` is bound by Odoo's cron machinery to
    ``self.env['account.asset']`` based on the ``model_id`` reference. The
    method (defined in ``models/account_asset.py``, decorated with
    ``@api.model``) handles:

      * **Batch processing**   a single ORM ``search`` retrieves all open
        assets with at least one due draft depreciation line; per-asset
        and per-line filtering uses Python ``filtered`` on already-loaded
        recordsets so the cron avoids N+1 queries even with thousands of
        assets in the portfolio.
      * **Idempotency** (AM-004 AC6 "Already Depreciated Error")   each
        depreciation line's ``action_post`` is a no-op when state != 'draft';
        re-running the cron after a partial failure (or running it twice
        manually via the "Run Manually" button) does not create duplicate
        journal entries. This property is verified by
        ``tests/test_am_004.py``.
      * **Fault tolerance** (AM-004 AC6)   per-line ``UserError`` (locked
        period, missing accounts, etc.) is caught, logged at WARNING, and
        the next line in the same asset is processed. Per-asset
        ``Exception`` is caught with ``_logger.exception`` so one asset's
        failure cannot abort the rest of the batch.
      * **Auto-close** (AM-004 AC6 "Fully Depreciated Asset")   after
        posting the due lines for an asset, if the recomputed
        ``net_book_value`` is at or below ``salvage_value``, the asset is
        transitioned to ``state='close'`` automatically with a chatter
        notice and an activity log entry.
      * **Structured logging** (AM-004 AC5)   emits INFO log records for
        cron start (asset count, today's date), cron completion
        (success/skipped/errors counters), WARNING records for each
        skipped line with the reason, and EXCEPTION records (with full
        traceback) for each errored asset.

    Schedule Rationale (per AM-004 Scheduled Action Considerations)
    ===============================================================
    Daily cadence (interval_number=1, interval_type='days') guarantees
    every scheduled depreciation date is processed exactly once per day.
    The 02:00 execution time is the canonical low-traffic window observed
    in core ``addons/account/data/service_cron.xml`` and avoids daytime
    user impact. The ``nextcall`` eval expression schedules the FIRST run
    at 02:00 *tomorrow* (today's date at 02:00 plus one day) so the cron
    does not fire immediately during module install/upgrade; subsequent
    runs occur every 24 hours at the same time of day.

    Field Notes (Odoo 19.0 ir.cron schema)
    ======================================
    * ``ir.cron`` uses ``_inherits = {'ir.actions.server': ...}`` in
      Odoo 19.0, so ``name``, ``model_id``, ``state``, and ``code`` are
      delegated fields from ``ir.actions.server``.
    * ``user_id``, ``active``, ``interval_number``, ``interval_type``,
      ``nextcall``, and ``priority`` are direct fields on ``ir.cron``.
    * The legacy ``numbercall`` and ``doall`` fields have been REMOVED
      from ``ir.cron`` in Odoo 19.0 (verified against
      ``odoo/addons/base/models/ir_cron.py`` and the live ``ir_cron``
      PostgreSQL table schema). Including ``<field name="numbercall">``
      or ``<field name="doall">`` would cause module install to fail
      with a missing-field error. Cron repetition is now controlled
      solely by the ``active`` boolean (unlimited runs are implicit
      while ``active=True``); the legacy "thundering herd" catch-up
      semantics formerly governed by ``doall`` are now handled
      internally by the cron executor reading the live ``nextcall``
      field at each scheduling cycle.
    * ``priority`` defaults to 5 in Odoo 19.0; an explicit value of 5 is
      set below to make the priority intent explicit and to match the
      schema's exposed members. Lower number = higher priority (0 is
      highest urgency, 10 is lowest).

    The ``<data noupdate="1">`` wrapper preserves administrator
    customizations of the cron (e.g., schedule time via ``nextcall``,
    interval changes, active toggle, user override) across module
    upgrades. Without this attribute, a module upgrade would overwrite
    administrator-configured schedules with the static defaults declared
    here.

    Rules Compliance (AAP Section 0.7)
    ==================================
    * R-01 (Module Independence): no references to sibling new modules
      (account_budget_management, account_deferred_revenue,
      account_payment_followup)   this XML file uses only the local
      external ID ``model_account_asset`` and the core ``base.user_root``
      reference.
    * R-02 (No Enterprise Dependencies): no references to Enterprise
      addons (account_asset, account_accountant, account_reports,
      account_budget, account_followup, account_deferred_revenue).
    * R-03 (N/A): this is XML data, not Python model code; ``_inherit``
      and ``_name`` are model-class concerns.
    * R-05 (N/A): this file declares an ``ir.cron`` data record, not a
      Python field on a core model.
    * R-06 (MUST   ir.cron via XML): cron declared here in XML, NOT via
      Python scheduling primitives. Compliance verified by static grep
      audit of the module: zero hits for ``threading.Timer``,
      ``APScheduler``, or ``sched.``.
    * R-07 (N/A here): this XML file contains no Python ``sudo()`` calls.
      The ``_cron_post_depreciation_entries`` method in
      ``models/account_asset.py`` is responsible for R-07 compliance
      and contains zero ``sudo()`` calls (the cron's elevated privilege
      is delivered via ``user_id ref="base.user_root"`` below, not via
      runtime privilege escalation).
    * R-09 (Exact folder name): file resides in
      ``addons/account_asset_management/data/``   folder name matches
      AAP Rule R-09 character-for-character.

    Precedent
    =========
    Pattern mirrors ``addons/account/data/service_cron.xml`` (Odoo core
    canonical ``ir_cron_auto_post_draft_entry`` precedent) for the
    ``state='code'`` + ``model._method()`` invocation style and the
    ``DateTime.now().replace(hour=2, minute=0) + timedelta(days=1)``
    eval expression for the next-day 02:00 ``nextcall`` schedule. The
    ``<data noupdate="1">`` wrapping pattern follows
    ``addons/mail/data/ir_cron_data.xml`` (OCA preferred convention so
    that administrator customizations are preserved across upgrades).
-->
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">

        <record id="ir_cron_asset_depreciation" model="ir.cron">
            <field name="name">Assets: Post Depreciation Entries</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="model_account_asset"/>
            <field name="state">code</field>
            <field name="code">model._cron_post_depreciation_entries()</field>
            <field name="user_id" ref="base.user_root"/>
            <field name="interval_number">1</field>
            <field name="interval_type">days</field>
            <field name="nextcall"
                   eval="(DateTime.now().replace(hour=2, minute=0, second=0) + timedelta(days=1)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="priority">5</field>
        </record>

    </data>
</odoo>
